Reliance Industries Ltd (RIL) shares surged nearly 3 percent on Thursday, emerging among the top gainers on the Nifty 50 after its telecom and digital subsidiary Jio Platforms announced the price band for its upcoming Rs 30,213-crore initial public offering (IPO). The proposed listing, set to become India's largest-ever IPO, has also strengthened expectations of value unlocking and a potential re-rating of RIL, with brokerages maintaining bullish views on the conglomerate.
 RIL shares climbed 2.8 percent to Rs 1,207 in late-morning trade, taking the company's market capitalisation to Rs 16.32 lakh crore. The gains followed Jio Platforms' announcement of a price band of Rs 1,065-1,119 per share for its IPO, which will open for subscription on October 21 and close on October 23.
At the upper end of the price band, Jio Platforms would raise Rs 30,213 crore through a fresh issue of 27 crore shares, surpassing Hyundai Motor India's Rs 27,859-crore IPO in 2024. The issue values Jio Platforms at approximately Rs 10.3 lakh crore, or $108 billion.
Unlike several previous mega IPOs, Jio's offering will comprise entirely fresh shares, with no offer for sale by existing shareholders. Of the net proceeds, up to Rs 27,500 crore will be used to repay borrowings of Reliance Jio Infocomm, the company's material subsidiary. Anchor investor bidding is scheduled for October 19, while the shares are expected to list on October 28.
 Share Markets Live Updates | Sensex, Nifty, GIFT Nifty Today 
Religare Broking believes the proposed Jio Platforms listing could become a major catalyst for a re-rating of Reliance Industries by establishing an independent market valuation for its largest growth asset and improving transparency in the conglomerate's sum-of-the-parts (SOTP) valuation.
The brokerage has assigned a 'Buy' rating to RIL, with a target price of Rs 1,696, implying an upside of around 40.5 percent from Thursday's trading price of Rs 1,207. According to Religare, independent price discovery for Jio could help narrow the holding-company discount for RIL stock. The brokerage recommends accumulating RIL shares ahead of Jio's listing rather than waiting for its market debut.
Motilal Oswal Financial Services has also highlighted the potential for value unlocking through Jio Platforms' listing, saying that RIL's stock price already factors in a substantial holding-company discount on its digital business. In a recent note, the brokerage estimated that RIL's valuation reflected an 18-36 percent discount on its approximately 66.4 percent stake in Jio Platforms, depending on the valuation multiple assigned to Reliance Retail Ventures.
The brokerage has maintained its 'Buy' rating on RIL, with a SOTP-based target price of Rs 1,530, representing an upside of approximately 26.8 percent from Thursday's trading price. Motilal Oswal said RIL would continue to offer exposure to its retail, energy and emerging businesses even after investors gain the option of directly investing in Jio Platforms.
The proposed use of IPO proceeds to reduce borrowings is another important element of the investment case for RIL.
Religare Broking expects debt repayment at Reliance Jio Infocomm to lower interest expenses, improve net profit margins and accelerate free cash flow generation. It also sees higher average revenue per user (ARPU), 5G premiumisation and digital services as key earnings drivers.
Motilal Oswal expects the case for a telecom tariff hike to strengthen following Jio's IPO and Vodafone Idea's proposed fundraise. It has factored in a smartphone tariff increase of around 15 percent in December 2026, which it believes could support approximately 15 percent EBITDA compound annual growth over FY26-29.
Beyond Jio, Religare expects Reliance Retail to benefit from store expansion and digital commerce growth; and oil-to-chemicals business to gain from improving refining economics and a petrochemicals recovery. The group's investments in solar, energy storage and hydrogen offer longer-term growth opportunities.
Motilal Oswal also identified stronger refining and petrochemical earnings, alongside a recovery in retail revenue growth and profitability, as important medium-term triggers for RIL.
