Foreign institutional investors (FIIs) sold Indian equities worth Rs 5,285.37 crore on Thursday, as their 15th straight session of net selling. Domestic institutional investors (DIIs) bought Rs 3,319.39 crore, according to exchange provisional data.
FIIs bought shares worth Rs 12,735.59 crore and sold Rs 18,020.96 crore. DIIs bought Rs 19,438.65 crore and sold Rs 16,119.26 crore.
The outflow was the fourth-largest of October, after Rs 12,943.58 crore on October 8, Rs 9,484.22 crore on October 1 and Rs 6,121.37 crore on October 7. It was also larger than the combined FII selling of Rs 4,871.05 crore over the previous three sessions.
 October FII selling nears Rs 50,000 crore 
The latest outflow takes October’s cumulative FII selling to Rs 49,934.93 crore. FIIs have been net sellers in all 10 sessions this month.
DIIs have been net buyers in all 10 sessions, with cumulative buying of Rs 51,829.41 crore. That is about 104% of FII selling for the month.
The combined FII-DII flow for October stands at a positive Rs 1,894.48 crore.
On Thursday, DII buying covered about 63% of FII selling. The day’s combined flow was a negative Rs 1,965.98 crore, the first negative reading since October 8.
On a year-to-date basis, FIIs have sold a net Rs 4.50 lakh crore, while DIIs have bought Rs 6.90 lakh crore .
The Sensex rose 1,533.72 points, or 2.11%, to 74,315.61. The Nifty 50 gained 374.65 points, or 1.66%, to 22,997.35. Gains in IT shares, softer oil prices and a stronger rupee supported the benchmarks. Broader markets ended weak.
