Buying was concentrated in large-cap stocks, supported by strong quarterly results from TCS and HCL Technologies and interest in attractively valued mega-cap stocks following recent corrections. However, broader-market participation remained weak, the Midcap 100 and Smallcap 100 declined 0.4% and 1.3%, respectively, indicating continued selling pressure and profit-booking beyond large caps.
"On the regulatory front, SEBI’s Secondary Market Advisory Committee has reportedly favoured retaining the existing 30-minute volume-weighted average price (VWAP) methodology for derivatives expiry settlement for at least a year, putting the proposed CAS-linked formula on hold. The panel has also recommended changes to market timings, while the treatment of market orders remains under review. India’s direct tax collections continued to grow, with gross collections rising 14.9% YoY to Rs 16.03 lakh crore and net collections increasing 13.2% to Rs 13.37 lakh crore as of 14 October. Net corporate tax collections rose 20.6% to Rs 5.99 lakh crore, while non-corporate tax collections increased 5.8% to Rs 6.91 lakh crore. STT collections surged 51.6% to Rs 46,840 crore, while refunds increased 24.2% to Rs 2.65 lakh crore. Cumulative direct tax collections had reached 49.6% of the FY27 Budget target, with corporate taxes leading the growth," he added.
