 Gold tax varies by form, holding period, and acquisition method.
 Physical gold and ETFs face 12.5% LTCG tax after specific terms.
 Inherited or gifted gold has unique tax rules and exemptions.
 Did our AI summary help? 
 Market Mastery
Gold investors should understand the tax implications before selling their holdings, as the tax treatment of gold varies depending on how it is held—for example, whether it is a gift from a grandmother or an investment in ETFs.
Buying, receiving, or selling gold, as well as reinvesting the proceeds, may attract taxes, although certain exemptions and reliefs are available under the tax rules. The tax treatment also varies depending on when and how the gold was acquired, how long it was held, its cost of acquisition, and whether any exemption applies.
 Why gold investors should track capital gains tax changes 
Long-term capital gains (LTCG) and short-term capital gains (STCG) taxes apply to profits earned from selling capital assets, such as listed shares, equity-oriented mutual funds, debt mutual funds, gold, property and bonds, subject to the relevant tax provisions. Rates and holding periods vary by asset class.
Securities transaction tax (STT), in contrast, is a transaction-based levy on specified securities transactions. STT generally does not apply to physical gold, gold jewellery or gold ETFs.
 Show 
 Gold-related investments may be taxed differently depending on how they are traded. Against this backdrop, even a modest change in gold taxation rates or indexation provisions can affect individuals holding gold as a long-term investment.
Here’s how different types of gold investments are taxed in India when sold.
Aditya Bhattacharya, Partner, King Stubb & Kasiva, Advocates and Attorneys, explains that since the July 2024 changes, now carried into the Income-tax Act, 2025, physical gold, jewellery and gold mutual funds become long-term after 24 months and are taxed at 12.5 percent (plus 4 percent cess) with no indexation.
Physical gold and digital gold include jewellery, coins, and bars, while digital gold lets investors buy gold online in small amounts without holding it physically. Platforms such as PhonePe and Paytm have offered digital gold services.
Gold Exchange-Traded Funds (ETFs) track domestic gold prices and trade on stock exchanges like shares. Listed gold ETFs are taxed at the same rate after 12 months. Shorter holdings are taxed at slab rates.
“Without indexation, a holder pays tax on the full nominal gain from gold ETFs, including the part that only offsets inflation. Even a one-point rise in the rate, or a longer holding period, would therefore hit hardest those who bought years ago and have seen large paper gains,” Bhattacharya said.
Gold mutual funds typically invest in gold ETFs, allowing investors to gain exposure to gold price movements without buying the metal directly.
Sovereign Gold Bonds (SGBs) are issued by the Reserve Bank of India on behalf of the Government of India. SGBs are denominated in grams of gold and offer returns linked to gold prices and a fixed interest rate under the bond terms.
SBGs were the most tax-efficient route, but Budget 2026 narrowed this. From April 1, 2026, the maturity exemption applies only to investors who subscribed at the original issue and hold to redemption; secondary-market buyers lose it.
Inherited gold includes jewellery, coins and bars received from family members through inheritance or under a will. Gold inherited or gifted by a specified relative is not taxed on receipt, but when it is sold the previous owner's cost and holding period carry over.
“A family heirloom with a very low original cost can therefore produce a large taxable gain, and without purchase records the taxman may treat the cost as nil or low. A tweak to long-term rates or holding periods would apply fully to such gold. Documentation of cost is the most practical protection," Bhattacharya said
Gold received as gifts includes jewellery, coins and bars received from relatives or others on occasions such as weddings and festivals. Gifts from specified relatives and gold received on the occasion of the recipient’s marriage are generally tax-exempt, while gifts from non-relatives are taxed at slab rate if total gifts in the year exceed Rs 50,000.
Notably, the Rs 1.25 lakh annual exemption applies only to listed equity gains, not gold. Long-term gains from gold can also be sheltered by reinvesting the net proceeds in a residential house, subject to conditions.
